WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:03.562
In this lesson you'll learn how old

2
00:00:03.562 --> 00:00:05.780
the tools you build with really are,

3
00:00:05.780 --> 00:00:08.148
and why their age matters.

4
00:00:08.148 --> 00:00:10.261
Almost everything under your AI

5
00:00:10.261 --> 00:00:12.583
tools is decades old,

6
00:00:12.583 --> 00:00:15.799
and only the AI layer on top is new.

7
00:00:15.799 --> 00:00:18.701
The terminal, files, the web,

8
00:00:18.701 --> 00:00:21.255
git: old and settled.

9
00:00:21.255 --> 00:00:25.481
The AI tools: a few years old, and still changing.

10
00:00:25.481 --> 00:00:28.535
So you'll know what to learn deeply once,

11
00:00:28.535 --> 00:00:30.789
and what to keep checking.

12
00:00:31.239 --> 00:00:34.710
On the course map, this lesson sits at the Start,

13
00:00:34.710 --> 00:00:37.915
in Orientation: what this course is,

14
00:00:37.915 --> 00:00:40.005
and the lantern you carry.

15
00:00:40.005 --> 00:00:42.199
Before you walk into the six districts,

16
00:00:42.199 --> 00:00:44.556
it helps to know their ages.

17
00:00:44.556 --> 00:00:46.808
Most of these streets were laid

18
00:00:46.808 --> 00:00:49.362
long before AI tools arrived.

19
00:00:49.362 --> 00:00:53.183
The lanterns, the AI, were strung up last.

20
00:00:53.634 --> 00:00:56.002
Aaron, who made this course, has a degree

21
00:00:56.002 --> 00:00:58.812
in Information Systems and Data Science.

22
00:00:58.812 --> 00:01:00.855
He still wrote Python in Notepad,

23
00:01:00.855 --> 00:01:04.362
and saved it as a .txt file.

24
00:01:04.362 --> 00:01:07.160
He didn't know to rename it to .py,

25
00:01:07.160 --> 00:01:10.956
or to open a terminal and cd into the folder.

26
00:01:10.956 --> 00:01:13.476
He'd never heard of .venv.

27
00:01:13.476 --> 00:01:15.055
None of that was new.

28
00:01:15.055 --> 00:01:18.317
Notepad came with the first Windows, in 1985.

29
00:01:18.317 --> 00:01:20.256
Python is from 1991.

30
00:01:20.256 --> 00:01:23.182
The Unix shell, from 1971.

31
00:01:23.182 --> 00:01:27.861
And the tool behind .venv joined Python in 2012.

32
00:01:27.861 --> 00:01:29.660
Aaron wasn't behind on the latest thing.

33
00:01:29.660 --> 00:01:31.378
He was missing the oldest things,

34
00:01:31.378 --> 00:01:34.387
the ones everyone assumes you already have.

35
00:01:34.836 --> 00:01:36.461
Picture one long street,

36
00:01:36.461 --> 00:01:38.958
with a year painted on every building.

37
00:01:38.958 --> 00:01:41.407
1969: Unix.

38
00:01:41.407 --> 00:01:43.509
1973: Ethernet,

39
00:01:43.509 --> 00:01:46.179
cables joining the computers in one building.

40
00:01:46.179 --> 00:01:49.000
1981: MS-DOS.

41
00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:52.298
1983: DNS, the internet's address book.

42
00:01:52.298 --> 00:01:55.433
Then the web, Python and Linux, all around 1991.

43
00:01:55.433 --> 00:01:57.348
JavaScript, 1995.

44
00:01:57.348 --> 00:01:58.869
Git, 2005.

45
00:01:58.869 --> 00:02:00.645
GitHub, 2008.

46
00:02:00.645 --> 00:02:02.491
Docker, 2013.

47
00:02:02.491 --> 00:02:04.221
VS Code, 2015.

48
00:02:04.221 --> 00:02:06.009
And only at the very end of the street,

49
00:02:06.009 --> 00:02:07.820
the lanterns. GitHub Copilot,

50
00:02:07.820 --> 00:02:10.862
suggesting code in your editor, 2021.

51
00:02:10.862 --> 00:02:12.754
ChatGPT, late 2022.

52
00:02:12.754 --> 00:02:16.203
MCP, a standard way to plug AI into other tools,

53
00:02:16.203 --> 00:02:17.944
November 2024.

54
00:02:17.944 --> 00:02:19.836
More than fifty years of street.

55
00:02:19.836 --> 00:02:22.044
A few years of lanterns.

56
00:02:22.494 --> 00:02:25.513
Lay those years on the road from the last lesson.

57
00:02:25.513 --> 00:02:29.147
Your computer: the shell, files, Python.

58
00:02:29.147 --> 00:02:30.482
Decades old.

59
00:02:30.482 --> 00:02:32.397
The network and the internet:

60
00:02:32.397 --> 00:02:35.242
Ethernet, DNS, the web.

61
00:02:35.242 --> 00:02:36.589
Decades too.

62
00:02:36.589 --> 00:02:39.630
Services like GitHub, Cloudflare and Stripe:

63
00:02:39.630 --> 00:02:41.755
mostly around 2010.

64
00:02:41.755 --> 00:02:45.087
And AI, the last stop, is the newest by far.

65
00:02:45.087 --> 00:02:47.304
Here's where the picture breaks.

66
00:02:47.304 --> 00:02:50.508
Every date is a birthday, not a finish line.

67
00:02:50.508 --> 00:02:53.736
Python and git still get new versions every year;

68
00:02:53.736 --> 00:02:55.954
what's old is the idea.

69
00:02:55.954 --> 00:02:59.646
AI research, too, goes back to the 1950s.

70
00:02:59.646 --> 00:03:02.072
What's new is AI you can talk to,

71
00:03:02.072 --> 00:03:04.732
inside the tools you use.

72
00:03:05.183 --> 00:03:09.258
Knowing the ages explains things you'll meet.

73
00:03:09.258 --> 00:03:13.322
The C:\ at the start of a Windows terminal

74
00:03:13.322 --> 00:03:18.070
is a habit from MS-DOS, in the early 1980s.

75
00:03:18.070 --> 00:03:22.052
A git answer from ten years ago often still works.

76
00:03:22.052 --> 00:03:24.676
That's not luck; the street hasn't moved.

77
00:03:24.676 --> 00:03:28.473
But an old answer where print has no parentheses

78
00:03:28.473 --> 00:03:31.271
is Python 2, retired in 2020.

79
00:03:31.271 --> 00:03:33.918
Same street, older building.

80
00:03:33.918 --> 00:03:36.170
And an AI can be out of date about AI,

81
00:03:36.170 --> 00:03:37.970
because what it learned stops

82
00:03:37.970 --> 00:03:39.874
where its training ended.

83
00:03:39.874 --> 00:03:44.692
So: old street, old answers mostly hold.

84
00:03:44.692 --> 00:03:47.236
Lantern: check the date.

85
00:03:47.686 --> 00:03:49.323
That's why AI tools can feel

86
00:03:49.323 --> 00:03:51.018
strange to learn with.

87
00:03:51.018 --> 00:03:54.397
The newest layer assumes you know the oldest ones.

88
00:03:54.397 --> 00:03:58.866
A coding agent will run cd, write a .py file,

89
00:03:58.866 --> 00:04:03.824
make a .venv, and save its work with git.

90
00:04:03.824 --> 00:04:05.170
If you've never met those,

91
00:04:05.170 --> 00:04:07.817
you can't tell whether it did the right thing.

92
00:04:07.817 --> 00:04:09.872
The newest thing on the road spends

93
00:04:09.872 --> 00:04:12.624
its day on the oldest ground.

94
00:04:12.624 --> 00:04:16.037
AI is the lantern: it lights every district,

95
00:04:16.037 --> 00:04:19.336
but it doesn't walk the streets for you.

96
00:04:19.787 --> 00:04:22.283
You don't need to memorize a single date.

97
00:04:22.283 --> 00:04:23.943
Nobody will quiz you.

98
00:04:23.943 --> 00:04:25.638
Who invented what,

99
00:04:25.638 --> 00:04:28.634
and which old system won the argument, can wait.

100
00:04:28.634 --> 00:04:31.977
Keep one question for anything new you meet:

101
00:04:31.977 --> 00:04:34.718
is this street, or lantern?

102
00:04:34.718 --> 00:04:37.726
Street: learn it once.

103
00:04:38.491 --> 00:04:41.500
Lantern: check back often.

104
00:04:41.949 --> 00:04:43.052
Three things to keep.

105
00:04:43.052 --> 00:04:45.885
Most of what you build on is decades old.

106
00:04:45.885 --> 00:04:47.115
The shell, files,

107
00:04:47.115 --> 00:04:49.240
the web and git had decades to settle,

108
00:04:49.240 --> 00:04:51.144
so what you learn there lasts.

109
00:04:51.144 --> 00:04:54.151
The AI layer is brand new.

110
00:04:54.151 --> 00:05:00.177
ChatGPT is from 2022, MCP from 2024,

111
00:05:00.177 --> 00:05:01.767
and both keep changing,

112
00:05:01.767 --> 00:05:04.658
so check the date on anything about them.

113
00:05:04.658 --> 00:05:07.328
Being lost isn't being behind.

114
00:05:07.328 --> 00:05:09.024
Aaron had a degree and still

115
00:05:09.024 --> 00:05:10.684
missed the old basics.

116
00:05:10.684 --> 00:05:12.286
They were never new.

117
00:05:12.286 --> 00:05:14.528
Nobody handed them over.
